U _ {p} \propto v (T + E _ {0}) ^ {- 2. 6}, \tag {19}
$$ with $E_{0}$ the rest energy. This spectrum conforms to observations at high energies. Due to convection/energy-loss processes, its form at low energies ( $T \lesssim 300$ MeV) is unknown but also unimportant. The interstellar electron spectrum used has the form $$
\begin{array}{l} j _ {T} = \exp [ a (\ln T) ^ {2} + b \ln T + c ] \quad T \geq 2 0 0 \mathrm{MeV} \\ = d T ^ {- e} \quad T <   2 0 0 \mathrm{MeV}, \tag {20} \\ \end{array}
$$ with T expressed in units of GeV and a = -0.2929, b = -1.870, c = -2.672, d = 0.1349 and e = 0.9272. Above 200 MeV, this spectrum is derived from Rockstroh (1977); below 200 MeV it is extended in a power-law form. At the pole $(\theta = 0)$ we specified $\partial U_{p} / \partial \theta = 0$ , while at the inner boundary at $r = r_{\odot}$ we put the radial component of the streaming vector (5) equal to zero. This is equivalent to $\partial U_{p} / \partial r = 0$ at $r = r_{\odot}$ . At the neutral sheet $(\theta = \pi /2)$ we required that the $\theta$ -component of the streaming vector (5) must be zero, i.e., $$
